The prior implementation fails because it mutates/rebuilds frames through recursive merges and assignments that can attempt to place heterogeneous lists into typed columns. The replacement below avoids all cell-level pandas assignment during optimization.

Key improvements:

- Never modifies source values or inserts synthetic index columns.
- Converts values only into a separate evaluator-compatible string matrix.
- Preserves mixed types, missing values, shape, index, and row identity.
- Evaluates up to three bounded candidate layouts using exact serialized-row Trie reuse.
- Includes the required global frequency ordering based on `len(v) * count(v) * (count(v)-1)`.
- Adds a prefix-aware global alternative, which captures reuse between distinct values sharing text prefixes.
- Uses a conditional partition tree with factorized integer codes and row-specific column orderings.
- Honors merge adjacency and one-way dependency precedence for global candidates.
- Avoids per-character Python LCP loops.

